Found while closing karr #86 (ADR 0013), not caused by it, and deliberately left
out of that guard.

ADR 0013 refuses a YAML spelling libyaml and Go resolve differently, because the
document then fails its own MAC. With mac_only_encrypted set, the digest covers
encrypted values only, so an unencrypted leaf CANNOT break the MAC -- and the
guard is therefore not installed for such a document (Format::YAML::serialize
passes mac_covered => 0). Measured, sops 3.13.3, leaf mode_unencrypted: 0755:

  mac_only_encrypted = 0   sops -d exit 51 (MAC mismatch)   -> refused since #86
  mac_only_encrypted = 1   sops -d exit 0,  reads 493       -> written today

What remains in the second row: the file says 0755, sops reads the integer 493,
File::SOPS reads 755. Nothing fails; the two implementations simply disagree
about a value in a document neither of them complains about. Same for 0o10,
0x1f, 1_000, .inf, Null, TRUE and a bare 2015-01-01.

Refusing it would be a behaviour change for documents that work today, which is
why #86 stopped where the MAC stops. The open question is whether a value
divergence with no MAC symptom is worth a refusal, a warning, or nothing at all.

Note the same divergence exists on the READ path in principle, but not in
practice: sops normalises the spelling when IT writes (a plaintext mode: 0755
becomes 493 in its output, measured), so a sops-written document never carries
one.

Wire lane. Measured with sops 3.13.3, Crypt::Age 0.002.
